  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CO-Livelihoods Support 2 Refugee farming</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Uganda is currently hosting over 1.5 million refugees who are originally from South Sudan (63,3%), the Democratic Republic of Congo(32.4%) and from other nationalities including Somalia (4,3%). These refugees are hosted in 13 settlements in Northern (for the South Sudanese) and Western (for the Congolese) regions of Uganda.
The refugee population has constantly increased due to war and persecution in South Sudan and the Democratic Republic of the Congo and figures as documented by December 2022 indicate across all three settlements of Imvepi, Rhino and Bidibidi respectively 133. 336, 66.525 and 190,256 refugees respectively with estimation of children (0-11years old) constituting 40% of the total population.
Based on a need assessment carried out by Oxfam last year, it was found that the primary source of income for refugees continues to be humanitarian aid. The assessment revealed that among the refugee population, 52% of the refugees had acceptable food consumption scores while 35% had borderline and 13% had poor food consumption scores. 20% of the refugees had not adopted livelihood copying strategies, 35% had stressed Livelihood coping strategies, 25% had Crisis livelihood coping strategies and 20% had Emergency Coping Strategies.
Unfortunately, the recent decision by the World Food Programme to reduce food assistance by 60% will exacerbate the challenging food security situation in refugee settlements. The consequences ofthis reduction will disproportionately affect women and children, intensifying their vulnerability.
RESPONSE STRATEGY
In accordancewith the Uganda National Development Plan III, the ReHoPe Strategy, the Food Security and Livelihoods Strategies 2022-2025, and theSkilling Uganda BTVET Strategic Plan 2012/3 &#x2013; 2021, the projectaims to provide communities with resources to protect and start rebuilding livelihood assets. This project will further support the ongoing DANIDA SPII initiative, which incorporates the UN nexus approach in programming carried out by Oxfam and its partners in West Nile (Imvepi and Bidibidi settlements) with the goal of empoweringcrisis-affected populations, particularly women and youth, to have improved access to food, sustainable livelihood as well as increased resilience to climate shocks.
PROJECT GOAL
150 HH provided with resources to protect and start rebuilding livelihood assets.
OBJECTIVE
Men and women are supported with income generation activities based on their interest and market demands.</narrative>
  </description>
